WEBVTT

00:00:00.200 --> 00:00:04.360
Weak authentication makes account takeover easier through reused passwords,

00:00:04.580 --> 00:00:07.540
weak recovery flows, poor enrollment controls,

00:00:07.760 --> 00:00:11.840
or inconsistent enforcement across applications and administrative tools.

00:00:12.060 --> 00:00:17.260
MFA failures occur when multifactor authentication is missing, optional, bypassable,

00:00:17.480 --> 00:00:20.920
or undermined by weak recovery and reset processes.

00:00:21.140 --> 00:00:24.820
Exceptions need governance because attackers often target them.

00:00:25.040 --> 00:00:30.000
Session management controls whether authenticated access remains trustworthy after login.

00:00:30.220 --> 00:00:35.820
Tokens should be protected, rotated when needed, invalidated on logout or major changes,

00:00:36.040 --> 00:00:37.720
and resistant to replay.

00:00:37.940 --> 00:00:42.260
Credential attacks such as credential stuffing, password spraying,

00:00:42.480 --> 00:00:46.800
and brute force attempts require monitoring, rate limits, lockout logic,

00:00:47.020 --> 00:00:49.420
risk-based checks, and alerting.

00:00:49.640 --> 00:00:53.480
Use multifactor authentication for required and high-risk access.

00:00:53.700 --> 00:00:56.980
Apply secure password and authenticator management practices.

00:00:57.200 --> 00:01:00.240
Use session timeout controls and secure token handling.

00:01:00.460 --> 00:01:08.540
Selected CMMC mappings for this topic include IA.L2-3.5.1 - Identification;

00:01:08.760 --> 00:01:18.040
IA.L2-3.5.2 - Authentication; IA.L2-3.5.3 - MFA.
